    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and ARBs are both recommended as interchangeable first-line options for treating high blood pressure, but ARBs are generally preferred because they have fewer side effects.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "ARBs are generally preferred because they have fewer side effects"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Mechanism: ACE inhibitors (ending in \"-pril\") block the production of angiotensin II, while ARBs (ending in \"-sartan\") block angiotensin II from binding to its receptors."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Effectiveness: Both classes lower blood pressure and protect the kidneys equally well."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cough: ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent, dry cough due to a buildup of bradykinin. ARBs do not affect bradykinin and rarely cause a cough."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Tolerability: ARBs are better tolerated by most patients. ACE inhibitors carry a small risk of angioedema (severe swelling), which requires an immediate switch to an ARB."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared Risks: Both can cause high potassium levels (hyperkalemia) and should never be taken together. They are also unsafe during pregnancy."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, share:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "If you are experiencing specific side effects"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Any other health conditions (like kidney disease or diabetes)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can provide more tailored information."
      }
    ],
